As I am only now progressing from beginner to intermediate after
playing nethack off-and-on for a few years (perhaps over 6 months
total), you have my sympathy.

When I was a new player, I preferred melee classes, and settled on
playing Samurai regularly.  Like you, I focused on gaining experience
through combat, but I'd often die before reaching level 6 and my best
games only saw my character advance to 10.  After all, killing things
is the only way to increase the level of your character, and therefore
gain power, right?

That's a mistake.  In Nethack, when you reach a new dungeon level, the
level of the initially generated monsters is based on your character
level.  When I realized this, I stopped looking at monsters as XP
pi=C3=B1atas, and began to focus on other important aspects of survival.

Here are some things that have begun to work for me:

 - Wait a while before killing things yourself.  Instead, flee past
your pet and let your pet kill things.  This increases your pet's HP
and level (and it levels faster than you do... I learned this playing
a healer with a stethoscope).

 - If you're stuck, use Elbereth *before* you're almost dead.  I
learned about Elbereth when I was still a new player but I didn't
understand the value of scrawling it in the dust multiple times even
without a source of permanent engraving.  It will give you time and
space, and if you've been advancing your pet and keeping it close, it
will give said pet an opportunity to dispatch your foes.

 - The power of your class pales in comparison to the utility of your
items!  Learn to identify the magic items you find.  Engrave with
wands, price-test everything you can... and when you get to an altar,
use it religiously (ha ha).  If you didn't already know, identify
scrolls are the cheapest, and therefore easy to ID.  If you have a
blessed scroll of identify, or a scroll of identify + holy water, use
them to identify your ENTIRE stash.

 - Maybe try a meta-strategy to change the pace.  I've grown quite
fond of the protection racket.  I started doing it as a gnomish
healer, then moved up to gnomish wizard and even *gasp* elvish
wizard.  I've recently started accomplishing it with my old favorite:
the samurai.  The fact that it's possible for ANY class to survive to
minetown at level 1 while looting a couple thousand gold coins along
the way is a testament to the sheer power of Petzilla.  If you advance
slowly, using Elbereth and running when neccessary, #praying when weak
from hunger, and let your pet kill everything, the pet will be level 9
with 72 hit points by the second level of the mines.


Good luck, and happy hacking!


